00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.930
I mean, it's hard to describe.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.020
What is the conversation
you can have about that?

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.550
And I realized
what I really like

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.530
to be able to do is talk
about Site Reliability

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:17.920
Engineering at
Google, but I just

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.670
don't feel like I know enough
to have that conversation.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:25.060
I didn't feel like I could
really represent the whole Site

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.230
Reliability
Engineering philosophy

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:31.930
and approach to
reliability really well.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.620
So, then I hit on the idea of
maybe now's the time for me

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.260
to do this mission
control rotation

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:39.280
and really learn what
this is all about.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.950
So, that's what I did, and
I started on that in May--

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.850
and I guess it's July now so
I'm a couple of months in.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:49.780
And so, it's still
just the beginning.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.860
One of the things that I'm
excited to do while I'm

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.281
on this rotation is write about
it on the Google Cloud Platform

00:05:56.281 --> 00:05:56.780
Blog.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.870
So, I did that a couple of
weeks ago, I think, and now I'm

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:01.680
here talking to you
guys on the podcast.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that blog post was amazing.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:03.480
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:04.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I really loved it.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:05.679
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:06:05.679 --> 00:06:10.020
So, let's find out what is
a Site Reliability Engineer?

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.050
PAUL NEWSON: So, Site
Reliability Engineering

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:16.410
is, to quote Ben
Treynor, it's what

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.840
happens when you put a
software engineer in charge

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.870
of an operations function.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:24.667
So, the history
of the term-- he's

00:06:24.667 --> 00:06:26.750
the one who coined the
phrase, because they wanted

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:31.310
to have a phrase, or a
name, or a title for what

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.980
do we call these software
engineers who aren't building

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:36.390
features into the
product but are instead

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.326
concerned with making sure
the product can scale up

00:06:39.326 --> 00:06:41.200
and making sure that
the product is reliable,

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.440
and doing all of that in
a sustainable way that

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:47.810
doesn't burn people out.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:50.290
And that's what Site
Reliability Engineering

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.240
was meant to capture.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.580
Other companies may call it SRE.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.770
They may call it something else.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:59.040
Broadly, it is an
operations function.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.540
We think we add a
little twist to it that

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:04.340
at least was novel at the time.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.360
DevOps is also mixed in there.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.710
It's all in the we're
not building features.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:16.600
We're talking about how you do
something like this reliably,

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.420
and well, and scalably.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:21.540
So, that's what, in a
not so brief nutshell,

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.314
Site Reliability
Engineering is about.

00:07:24.314 --> 00:07:26.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's a role
that I really like at Google,

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.420
because it's-- I think very
different to what other people

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:31.880
expect from DevOps.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:35.657
I know that SRE people
write lots of code.

00:07:35.657 --> 00:07:37.240
They deal with a
great amount of code.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:41.245
But my favorite thing about
them is you have SRE jackets.

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:46.826
PAUL NEWSON: I don't
have an SRE jacket.

00:07:46.826 --> 00:07:48.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You
don't have it yet.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:49.590
You better get one.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:50.667
Those are amazing.

00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:53.000
PAUL NEWSON: Well, I'll tell
them you said so, Francesc,

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.000
so that now they
have to give me one,

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.060
because you said it
publicly on the podcast

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:58.004
that I should get one.

00:07:58.004 --> 00:07:58.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.320
PAUL NEWSON: So, maybe
that's just what I need.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:04.014
But yeah, there's a badge.

00:08:04.014 --> 00:08:04.930
It's a leather jacket.

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:05.840
You can choose your style.

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.965
There's a few different
styles, and there's a badge

00:08:07.965 --> 00:08:13.170
that you can put on it which
is modeled after the badges

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:15.900
that they would
have at NASA mission

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.489
control for each mission.

00:08:17.489 --> 00:08:19.780
They would have a mission
patch for each mission, which

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:20.920
I think the
astronauts would wear,

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.450
and then they would have a
mission patch for the mission

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:25.590
control people as well.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.040
And so, somebody
came up with the idea

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.000
long ago that, wow,
it would be really

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.510
cool to have that style of patch
on a jacket just to show, hey,

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:34.250
we're SREs.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:35.710
We keep Google up.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:37.585
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
those are really cool.

00:08:37.585 --> 00:08:39.130
And the patch has a slogan?

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:44.480
PAUL NEWSON: Yes, and I'm not
going to try and pronounce

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:45.496
the Latin, because--

00:08:45.496 --> 00:08:47.090
MARK MANDEL: Oh, come on, try.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:48.196
PAUL NEWSON: No, no.

00:08:48.196 --> 00:08:50.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Come
on, you're from Canada.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:54.310
They speak Latin
up there, I think.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:59.260
PAUL NEWSON: But it's
based on the motto

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.970
that Gene Kranz coined
for mission control, which

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:04.370
I believe is-- oh man.

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.090
You'd think I'd
have this by heart.

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Because
hope is not a strategy.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:08.749
Is it that one?

00:09:08.749 --> 00:09:10.290
PAUL NEWSON: That
is a different one.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:14.800
Hope is not a strategy is a
slogan-- is an unofficial SRE

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.350
slogan, but this one
is tough and competent.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, OK, cool.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.760
PAUL NEWSON: That was the
mission control slogan,

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.080
and that's what's
on the actual patch.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.950
But hope is not a
strategy is also

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:33.470
one of the things
that is an SRE slogan.

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:38.820
And the philosophy there being
is anything that can go wrong

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:40.190
will go wrong, pretty much.

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:43.280
And hoping it won't go wrong
is not a viable strategy

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.700
when you do things at the
scale that Google does.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:49.870
Because at some point, if
you have enough servers doing

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:52.120
enough things in
enough data centers,

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.640
then something that
happens once in a million

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.250
is happening
constantly at Google,

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:01.820
because we're doing millions and
millions of millions of things

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:03.420
all the time.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.440
So those very,
very rare bugs that

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:12.000
might hit a small service once
a year could hit us once a day

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.080
or once a minute.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:18.719
So, hope is not a strategy is a
very important slogan for SRE.

00:10:18.719 --> 00:10:21.010
MARK MANDEL: Sometimes you
even get struck by lightning

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:22.340
four times in a row.

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:25.220
PAUL NEWSON: Yes.

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:28.770
And then it happens
a second time.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.260
MARK MANDEL: But
one thing I love

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:32.260
about that is I just know
that there is probably

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:35.780
an SRE or a hardware ops person
somewhere who's like all right,

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:39.252
now we need to protect
us against [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:39.252 --> 00:10:41.460
There's someone with that
plan with a document saying

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.169
this is what we need to
prevent against going forward.

00:10:44.169 --> 00:10:46.210
PAUL NEWSON: So, you can't
predict every failure.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:48.080
That's an interesting thing.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:54.030
So, what you want to do is
have a culture and a process

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.570
for dealing with whatever it
is that comes up, because you

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:58.110
can't predict everything.

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:02.300
You're going to in fact--
this job would be extremely

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:06.950
boring if all we ever did was
respond to the same problems

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:08.290
over and over again.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:12.110
It's actually an explicit
goal that we should never

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:16.510
have the same problem twice,
because we had it once already.

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:19.520
We did a post-mortem on it, we
figured out why it happened,

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.010
and we fixed whatever that was.

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:25.460
So, every time the
pager goes off,

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:28.310
it should be a new and novel
thing you've never seen before.

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:30.560
So, that's what you
have to practice for.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.830
You can't practice for
the specific scenario.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:37.950
You have to work on things that
are generic across problems,

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:44.280
across incidents, like you
need a good instant response

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:47.442
method that people understand.

00:11:47.442 --> 00:11:49.150
I can't speak to that
right now because I

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:51.610
haven't learned that part.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:56.500
I'm currently embedded with the
Google Compute Engine SRE team.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:59.560
And one of my goals
for this quarter

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.090
is to be trained
up to go on call,

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:03.954
so hopefully I can go
on call next quarter.

00:12:03.954 --> 00:12:05.870
But currently, I don't
understand the incident

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.800
response process
that we have here.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:12.590
So, I'm not going to
pretend to say something

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:15.550
intelligent about it here on the
podcast, but I know it exists

00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:18.100
and I see it in
action and I watch.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:20.200
I was thinking this
morning, actually,

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:22.070
anticipating this
interview, and I

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:24.940
was thinking I feel like
we should have t-shirts

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:28.250
that say keep calm and SRE.

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:33.700
Because it's kind of this very--
even when stuff is going wrong,

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:40.120
people are pretty chill because
it's like this is the job.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.300
And people are set
up to succeed in it.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:44.570
You have the resources.

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:45.510
You have the backup.

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:50.000
You're not out
there by yourself,

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.130
and it happens all the time.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:56.030
MARK MANDEL: It's kind of like
being on call for-- like you're

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:58.290
part of emergency services.

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:01.870
Your job is to put out fires
or your job is to save people.

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:03.400
Your job is to
basically sit there

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:05.649
and say, OK, things are going
to go wrong all the time

00:13:05.649 --> 00:13:09.580
and our job is to make sure that
they go as best as they can.

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:14.220
PAUL NEWSON: And that's really
half the job, I would say.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:17.270
And this is a really,
really important thing

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:20.120
to understand about
SRE-- and probably I

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:23.740
would say more broadly modern
operations of large scale

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.270
services.

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:27.260
So, let's do a
thought experiment.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:32.490
You ship a service that runs
and it's running on 10 servers,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:36.700
and you need to pay
one person full time

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:42.410
to do manual things to those
servers to keep them up.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:45.460
We call that toil where
it's something that you do.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:46.390
It's necessary.

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:47.310
It's important.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.196
But basically what
you're doing is

00:13:49.196 --> 00:13:51.070
you're returning the
service to a good state.

00:13:55.535 --> 00:13:57.980
And now you get lots
of new customers

00:13:57.980 --> 00:13:59.500
and you have 20 servers.

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:02.440
Now you need two people
to do that, assuming

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:05.510
your toil scales with the
number of servers you have.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:07.480
Then let's say you
don't add servers.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:11.000
Let's say you have these really
sharp developers who come up

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.730
with these awesome
new features and they

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:17.350
deploy these new features, and
that increases the surface area

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:17.990
of the product.

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:20.180
The product got bigger,
even though it's not

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.800
more servers or more users.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.430
So, now you're growing
in multiple dimensions.

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:28.330
If you need to scale, the
number of people doing

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:32.680
toil on your service with
the number of servers,

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:34.880
or the number of users,
or the amount of traffic,

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:37.800
or the amount of features,
then eventually you're

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:41.370
going to have an ops team
which is unsustainably huge.

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:43.290
You won't be able to
hire enough people

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:45.100
to take care of your service.

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:49.920
So, I would almost say the
more important role of SRE--

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:54.260
we're well known for-- we carry
the pager for the services

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:55.650
we support.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:56.917
We are on the front lines.

00:14:56.917 --> 00:14:58.250
That's what everybody thinks of.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:14:59.958
Even internally at
Google, usually that's

00:14:59.958 --> 00:15:03.190
what everyone thinks of
when they think of SRE.

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:06.740
But we spend less than half
of our time doing that.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:09.790
And we spend more
than half of our time

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:13.220
writing code and doing
engineering work so

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:19.190
that the toil does not scale
with the size of the service.

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:23.810
Let's take Google Compute
Engine as an example.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:25.100
Our usage is growing.

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:27.760
That's probably not a secret.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.410
And we are constantly
launching new features.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:32.050
Just look at the
Cloud Platform Blog.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:33.740
So we're growing in
all the dimensions

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:35.130
that you can imagine.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:38.410
The product is growing
much, much, much faster

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:41.500
than the size of the
SRE team supporting it.

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:43.480
In fact, the size of the
SRE team supporting it

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:46.060
is roughly constant, if
you look at the feature set

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:49.620
that one team is supporting.

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:52.050
So, how is it possible
that the service can

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.410
get so much bigger
and the team that

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:58.180
is on the front lines on
call for it doesn't grow?

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:01.930
Well, that's because that
team is constantly doing work

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:06.570
to make the service
easier to keep up,

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:10.710
easier to monitor, easier so
that things that might have

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:13.250
happened a year ago that
required manual intervention--

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:14.570
it just happened now.

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:16.480
We have programs for that.

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:17.910
If it's something
that you can do

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:22.410
without really discerning
human judgment,

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:24.194
you just write a
program for that

00:16:24.194 --> 00:16:25.360
and then it does it for you.

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:26.590
If you know what
the solution is,

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:28.320
you just write a program
for it, and then you

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:29.830
deploy that program,
and then you

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:31.787
make sure that program's up.

00:16:31.787 --> 00:16:33.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
reminds me of the joke

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:36.890
if you're not good I'll
replace you with a script.

00:16:36.890 --> 00:16:38.180
It's something like that.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:40.380
Basically, that is your job.

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:41.950
PAUL NEWSON: Exactly.

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:46.710
We don't want to hire more SREs
to do the same thing that we're

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:47.304
doing.

00:16:47.304 --> 00:16:49.470
We want to write scripts
for what we're doing today,

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:53.690
and then go do something
interesting tomorrow

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:55.370
that we haven't done before.

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:56.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So,
talking about things

00:16:56.420 --> 00:16:58.669
that are interesting, you
said that you're not on call

00:16:58.669 --> 00:17:00.570
and that you're
doing all the things.

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.780
One of those things is actually
a pretty amazing thing.

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:07.800
It is a new region on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.079
PAUL NEWSON: That's correct.

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:13.170
I had the privilege of
being peripherally involved

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:18.890
in the unveiling of our new
us-west1 region this past week,

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:21.210
which was a really
great experience.

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:22.730
And I want to be
super clear here

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:25.349
that many, many,
many other people did

00:17:25.349 --> 00:17:27.790
tons, and tons, and tons
of work over many weeks

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:31.800
to actually bring
up a new region.

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:35.610
Servers need to actually arrive
at places and be put in racks,

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:39.660
and all kinds of services need
to be turned up and monitored

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:40.330
on those things.

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:47.980
And then the very, very, very
last step is we flick a switch,

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:50.060
and now it appears to
the world and everybody

00:17:50.060 --> 00:17:51.760
can see all this
hard work that's

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:53.590
happened over a period of time.

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:59.830
So, my part in that was I
got to be somewhat involved

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:02.450
in flipping the
switch, so to speak,

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:04.330
because something that
I'd been working on

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:08.810
was how we do configuration
changes for our Google Compute

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:10.370
Engine.

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:11.840
Let me step back a second.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:15.510
Any change you make to your
service carries some risk.

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:20.230
If you want a 100% reliable
service never change anything

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:21.912
and then it won't break.

00:18:21.912 --> 00:18:23.370
But that's not what
customers want.

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:24.600
Customers want new regions.

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:26.980
Customers want new features.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:29.795
We need to do these things,
because even if we tried

00:18:29.795 --> 00:18:31.420
to stay static, our
customers wouldn't.

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:33.570
They would bring
new workloads to us.

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:38.610
So, we're constantly modifying
and changing the system.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.030
And the interesting
part is how do you

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.510
do that as safely as possible?

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.620
So, I was involved
with, all right,

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:47.890
we're going to unveil
this new region.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:49.815
How do we want to do this?

00:18:49.815 --> 00:18:51.940
What are the series of
steps we want to go through.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:55.190
What are the checks and
balances along the way,

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:57.820
and how long do we want
to take, things like that.

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:02.130
And so, I got to be involved
in defining that process, which

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:04.480
was mostly just
writing down stuff

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:06.470
I'd learned from all
the people around me

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:08.464
in the past couple of months.

00:19:08.464 --> 00:19:10.130
But I just happened
to be the person who

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:13.950
was collecting that information
then presenting it as a plan.

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:16.750
And then people much more
knowledgeable and experienced

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:19.760
than I said, yes,
this plan makes sense.

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:20.290
Go do it.

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:21.960
And then I wasn't even the
one flipping the switch,

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.290
because if you're going to
modify a production service,

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:25.748
the person who
should be doing that

00:19:25.748 --> 00:19:27.000
is the person who's on call.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.930
So, I essentially
presented this plan

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:31.130
to the people who
happened to be on call,

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:34.200
who happened to be carrying the
pager at that moment in time.

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:36.408
I said, all right, here's
how we're going to do this,

00:19:36.408 --> 00:19:37.720
and then they went and did it.

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:39.600
So, it was lots of fun.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:41.380
I really enjoyed it.

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:45.580
Now, I want to get more involved
in how we turn up regions

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:50.520
and be involved in one
from beginning to end.

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.750
We've announced that we're
going to do a lot of these

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:53.970
over the next year or so.

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:56.280
So, there should be
lots of opportunity

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:58.145
for me to get involved in that.

00:19:58.145 --> 00:19:58.895
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:19:58.895 --> 00:20:01.269
I have a small question for
you from earlier, because I'm

00:20:01.269 --> 00:20:02.590
actually just curious.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:05.550
You talked about doing
a rotation through SRE.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:07.767
Do people normally do rotations?

00:20:07.767 --> 00:20:10.100
Do they come and they go, or
is it a permanent position?

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:11.520
How does that normally work?

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:14.090
PAUL NEWSON: So SRE is
usually a permanent position,

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:16.680
but we have this thing called
the mission control program,

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:20.090
because a lot of
software engineers

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:22.570
who have worked at other
companies come to Google

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:25.970
and they come to Google
as a software engineer.

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:30.610
And they may be
interested in how

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:34.350
to run a service at
really high scale

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:36.360
kind of like I was
when I came here,

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:41.170
but they may have come from a
culture where operations is not

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:46.280
as well regarded and where
the operations function is not

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:50.530
as well supported
as it is at Google.

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:54.730
So, they may be hesitant
to become an SRE.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:57.220
They might think, man, I
don't want to carry the pager.

00:20:57.220 --> 00:21:00.890
That just sounds like
it's going to be draining.

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:03.940
It's going to take
over my whole life.

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:07.630
So, this is a way for
them to come into SRE,

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:10.980
learn all about it, get
trained up, carry the pager,

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:13.120
and see if this is something
that they like to do.

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:15.770
And what often they
discover along the way is,

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:19.074
wow, real engineering
work happens here.

00:21:19.074 --> 00:21:20.740
I'm still writing
code most of the time,

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:22.860
but I'm also doing
these really cool things

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:26.302
with these huge systems
that I can't believe

00:21:26.302 --> 00:21:27.510
they let me touch this stuff.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:28.135
It's fantastic.

00:21:31.050 --> 00:21:32.500
And then they have
the experience

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:35.200
of carrying the pager and
they realize, oh, Google

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:38.740
understands how to make being
on call a sustainable thing that

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:40.770
doesn't ruin your life.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:43.230
We have the advantage of
being a global company.

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:46.070
So, the Google
Compute Engine team

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:48.590
that I'm embedded
with, for example,

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:51.310
is in our Seattle office,
but we have a sister team

00:21:51.310 --> 00:21:54.130
in the London office, which is
conveniently about eight time

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:55.220
zones away.

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:57.170
So, when we're sleeping
they're on call,

00:21:57.170 --> 00:21:58.970
and when they're
sleeping we're on call.

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:02.980
It makes it far more sustainable
than the typical you're

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:03.520
the ops guy.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:04.280
Here's your pager.

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:05.742
It could ring at any time.

00:22:05.742 --> 00:22:06.450
Good luck to you.

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:09.530
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I still
have stress-induced dreams

00:22:09.530 --> 00:22:11.630
of getting woken up at
4 o'clock in the morning

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:15.080
to battle some fire that
happens on some server.

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:18.537
PAUL NEWSON: Right, and
that's not how we do it here,

00:22:18.537 --> 00:22:19.745
because it's not sustainable.

00:22:22.386 --> 00:22:29.070
Our management in SRE views SREs
as a rare and valuable resource

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:33.260
that-- it's hard to
hire into this position.

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:35.490
And once we have somebody
in this position,

00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:37.300
we want to take care
of them and make sure

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.200
that they want to
stay in that position,

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:41.100
because we can't
possibly hire enough.

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:42.600
This is the other
reason why we need

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:45.390
to make sure that we don't need
to scale the number of SREs

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:48.290
with the size of our services,
because we couldn't possibly

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:51.750
hire enough, if that's
what we were doing.

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:56.900
So, we have guidelines
around how many incidents

00:22:56.900 --> 00:23:00.570
an on-caller should be taken
care of in a particular shift.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:02.880
Then if it goes above a
certain threshold, that's

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:06.290
a warning sign that this
service is now not sustainable

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:10.350
and we need to stop
focusing on features

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:14.110
or shift our focus from
launching new features to make

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:16.620
this a more sustainable service,
because if we don't, it's

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:18.450
going to run away.

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:20.730
These feel like the
slippery slopes.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:26.740
If once you get a certain
velocity of unmanageability,

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:28.150
then suddenly
you're spending all

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:30.360
of your time trying to deal
with the unmanageability

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.150
and you can't make
progress anymore.

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:35.430
So, we have these metrics which
we review on a regular basis.

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:37.960
How many incidents are
we seeing per shift?

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:40.730
Are our engineers
spending a majority

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:44.840
of their time
actually doing work

00:23:44.840 --> 00:23:47.800
to make the service more
maintainable, more sustainable

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:51.720
versus just doing manual toil?

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:55.420
And so, coming back to the
mission control rotation

00:23:55.420 --> 00:23:57.500
you get into SRE
and you realize,

00:23:57.500 --> 00:24:00.300
wow, Google has figured
out how to make this work.

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.730
And not only that, this is fun.

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:03.870
This is rewarding.

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:05.790
I like the people
I'm doing it with.

00:24:05.790 --> 00:24:06.377
This is great.

00:24:06.377 --> 00:24:07.460
I want to keep doing this.

00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:08.880
I'm not scared of it anymore.

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:13.670
So, roughly half the software
engineers who come through--

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:15.810
and software engineer
from a development team

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:17.810
who previously had been
working on launching new

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:22.690
features roughly speaking who
come through a mission control

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:26.820
rotation, they choose to stay
as SREs after the rotation

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:28.795
and that becomes
their permanent job.

00:24:28.795 --> 00:24:30.170
There's other
people who are just

00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:33.130
hired straight into SRE because
they were doing SRE-like work

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:34.830
at another company.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:36.720
Or maybe they just
take the plunge

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:38.380
and they say I
really want to get

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:41.380
my hands on these huge services
and they make the plunge.

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:43.940
But to answer your question,
the mission control rotation

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:46.170
program is officially
a rotation program

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:47.510
that lasts for six months.

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:50.870
But really what it is is a
chance to experience SRE,

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:52.850
and then you get to make
a choice at the end.

00:24:52.850 --> 00:24:55.360
SRE would probably
love to have you stay,

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:59.810
but you can always go back to
a typical development position

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:00.550
as well.

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:02.640
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Or
developer advocacy.

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:04.700
PAUL NEWSON: Or in my
case, developer advocacy.

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:06.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
We want you back.

00:25:06.150 --> 00:25:07.650
MARK MANDEL: I was
going to say, now

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:09.749
I'm a little worried
we're going to lose you.

00:25:09.749 --> 00:25:12.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I have a couple
of questions regarding this.

00:25:12.290 --> 00:25:15.310
The first one is you said that
you still write a lot of code.

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:17.480
And you know my question now.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:18.980
What language?

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:22.010
PAUL NEWSON: Oh, well you'll be
happy to hear, Francesc, that I

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:23.800
am currently learning Go.

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:25.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There you go.

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:27.920
PAUL NEWSON: Because
the system that I

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:33.390
will be enhancing with new
features is written in Go.

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:34.770
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:25:34.770 --> 00:25:38.930
PAUL NEWSON: And Go is a very
popular language among SREs

00:25:38.930 --> 00:25:40.940
for all reasons I
probably don't need

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:43.760
to tell you, Francesc, other
than just being awesome.

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:48.980
It's also very fast, but it's
because previously Python

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:53.030
was very popular among SREs,
mostly because of that really

00:25:53.030 --> 00:25:54.850
fast iterative development.

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:57.910
And with Go, you
get that but it's

00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:00.260
not an interpreted language,
which personally I'm

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:01.680
going to expose my bias now.

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:05.340
I really like languages that
actually compile to something

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:10.210
and where types have meaning,
and I'm not getting type errors

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:12.360
at runtime-- my personal bias.

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:16.540
Use the tools you like, but
I'm a C programmer at heart,

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:18.870
so Go makes me very, very happy.

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:20.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And you were saying

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:24.380
that it is hard to
hire SREs, and that

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:27.380
is why when Google
hires SREs, we

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:29.680
try to keep them as
happy as possible.

00:26:29.680 --> 00:26:31.980
What are the skills
you think are

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:35.940
the most important to have to
become an SRE or to be a good

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:36.810
SRE?

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:40.545
PAUL NEWSON: Well, I'm going
to try an accurately repeat

00:26:40.545 --> 00:26:42.420
things I've heard others
say, because I don't

00:26:42.420 --> 00:26:43.970
feel I'm really
qualified to give

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:45.660
my own answer to this question.

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:51.710
But we sort of have two types
of people who typically come in

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:54.600
and interview for SRE
and are successful

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:56.470
and become successful SREs.

00:26:56.470 --> 00:27:01.750
One is what you would expect
from pretty much applying

00:27:01.750 --> 00:27:05.926
to a software engineering role
at Google, same skill set.

00:27:05.926 --> 00:27:07.050
You're a software engineer.

00:27:07.050 --> 00:27:08.174
You know how to write code.

00:27:08.174 --> 00:27:10.470
You know how to
architect systems.

00:27:10.470 --> 00:27:13.060
Hopefully you have some
distributed systems knowledge.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:16.754
Those people can come in
and become successful SREs.

00:27:16.754 --> 00:27:18.170
And, in fact, most
people that are

00:27:18.170 --> 00:27:19.920
coming through the
mission control program

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:22.560
fit that profile.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:27.120
Then there's what they
call the system engineer.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:28.720
That's what they call it.

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:34.660
And these are people who may
not have the formal software

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:36.200
engineering background.

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:38.900
Maybe they didn't graduate from
a computer science or computer

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:41.050
engineering program,
but they have

00:27:41.050 --> 00:27:45.830
been architecting and building
and operating large scale

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:49.490
systems as part of their work
and part of their career.

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:51.670
And they come in with
a particular mindset

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:55.670
and a set of experiences,
which is super valuable.

00:27:55.670 --> 00:27:59.900
And so, there's a path for
them as well within SRE,

00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:04.500
both in terms of being hired
and progressing in their career.

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:07.610
And what we've found
is that it really

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:11.580
doesn't matter which of
those two profiles you meet.

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:14.720
These people who may not have
the formal software engineering

00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:17.140
or computer science
background, they're

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:18.515
still writing code today.

00:28:18.515 --> 00:28:20.890
Lots of people write code who
don't have that background,

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:22.973
and there's lots of people
who do it very well who

00:28:22.973 --> 00:28:25.010
don't have that background.

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:28.680
And they come in and they make
equally good contributions,

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:31.970
but they also tend to
bring a set of experiences

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:37.820
and knowledge of low level
systems and often networking,

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:39.835
which is super useful to SRE.

00:28:39.835 --> 00:28:44.240
And then these two
populations mix together

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:48.542
and everyone learns from each
other, and it's fantastic.

00:28:48.542 --> 00:28:50.250
MARK MANDEL: Cool,
all right, well, we're

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:51.916
start to run out of
time, unfortunately.

00:28:51.916 --> 00:28:53.770
This is a great conversation.

00:28:53.770 --> 00:28:56.380
But before we run
off, is there anything

00:28:56.380 --> 00:28:58.245
you'd like to
mention, or touch on,

00:28:58.245 --> 00:29:00.245
or something we may have
missed in our questions

00:29:00.245 --> 00:29:01.962
that you want to say?

00:29:01.962 --> 00:29:04.284
PAUL NEWSON: Oh, let's see.

00:29:04.284 --> 00:29:05.700
Oh, yeah, there's
a couple things.

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:08.465
One is take everything
you've heard here

00:29:08.465 --> 00:29:10.090
with a grain of salt.
This is obviously

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:12.490
just my opinion based on
a few weeks of hanging out

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:15.450
with these people and
I'm not an expert on SRE,

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:18.860
so please bear that in mind.

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:20.780
But there's also a
couple of other resources

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:22.330
I'd like to point
people towards.

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:26.740
At SREcon14, I
believe, Ben Treynor

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:30.520
gave a talk where he walked
through the principles of SRE.

00:29:30.520 --> 00:29:33.340
And a great deal of what
I've told you here today

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:36.660
I learned from watching that
talk and other talks like it.

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:39.710
So, if this is an
interesting topic to you,

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:42.640
it would be very well worth
45 minutes of your time to go

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:44.480
watch Ben Treynor
talk about this

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:47.370
instead of listening to me.

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:50.220
And then if that really
whets your appetite

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:53.970
and you really want
to go deep, there

00:29:53.970 --> 00:29:57.130
was a book released recently
called "Site Reliability

00:29:57.130 --> 00:29:59.101
Engineering," and
that's a great read.

00:29:59.101 --> 00:30:00.600
I'm still working
my way through it.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:03.080
I don't know all
the material in it.

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:06.300
And I think there's a lot
of good wisdom in there.

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:10.440
It was written by people
who are SREs at Google.

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:13.420
It's a series of essays on a
series of important topics.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:16.240
I think there's a lot of
really good knowledge in there.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:20.430
So, that would be a
fantastic resource.

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:24.504
And then finally, if you
are interested in this topic

00:30:24.504 --> 00:30:26.170
but don't have the
time to read the book

00:30:26.170 --> 00:30:27.797
or can't set aside
the 45 minutes,

00:30:27.797 --> 00:30:29.880
just keep watching the
Google Cloud Platform Blog,

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:34.900
because I hope to publish more
little nuggets of my experience

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:38.820
there in a way that won't take
you hours and hours to consume

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:40.620
where I can introduce
some of the concepts

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:42.810
and some of the aha moments
I've had along the way,

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:43.810
and maybe you can share.

00:30:43.810 --> 00:30:46.600
Because I'm on this journey
from not understanding this

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:49.130
stuff to hopefully
understanding this stuff.

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:51.700
And I hope to publish
