MARK MANDEL: 100%.

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:53.400
But why do we get into our
question of the week which

00:31:53.400 --> 00:31:57.630
is looking at when you have a
container engine cluster set up

00:31:57.630 --> 00:31:59.490
on GKE.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:02.060
Obviously new versions
of Kubernetes come up

00:32:02.060 --> 00:32:04.140
and you want to upgrade to them.

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:05.970
How do you handle that?

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:08.490
I know part of it automated,
part of it is not.

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:10.385
How do you manually
process that through

00:32:10.385 --> 00:32:12.551
and make sure you have the
least amount of down time

00:32:12.551 --> 00:32:15.045
and manage that within your
schedule for your application?

00:32:15.045 --> 00:32:16.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, yeah, that
is a question that many people

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:18.060
ask quite often.

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:19.899
So, one of our
coworkers, Terry Ryan,

00:32:19.899 --> 00:32:21.440
wrote a blog post
about it, and we'll

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:24.720
have a link on
the show as usual.

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:27.960
But basically the answer
is, OK, so the master

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:31.990
of your-- the masters will
be upgraded automatically

00:32:31.990 --> 00:32:35.720
at some point not too long
after the new version has

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:37.140
been released.

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:39.250
The master will be
rebooted and you

00:32:39.250 --> 00:32:40.500
don't need to care about this.

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:41.590
Everything just happens.

00:32:41.590 --> 00:32:44.630
It's invisible, it's
magic, and it's beautiful.

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:47.010
Now, we don't do this for nodes.

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:47.720
Why?

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:50.590
Well, because imagine that
you are getting a huge amount

00:32:50.590 --> 00:32:52.830
of traffic and at
that moment we decide

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:56.040
that is a perfect moment to
restart your nodes-- not great.

00:32:56.040 --> 00:33:00.230
So, you can restart them
either by just restarting them.

00:33:00.230 --> 00:33:01.980
You will get a new
node, and that new node

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:04.700
will have the new
version of current IDs,

00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:10.419
or also you have a way
to upgrade manually.

00:33:10.419 --> 00:33:12.710
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, there's
a series of gcloud commands,

00:33:12.710 --> 00:33:13.680
like upgrade commands.

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:17.200
And you can also do it through
the web interface as well.

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:19.630
It says in our documentation
it says generally you

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:21.519
want to be upgrading
in a timely manner.

00:33:21.519 --> 00:33:23.310
Nodes that are more
than two minor versions

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:25.310
behind the master may
not work properly.

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:27.750
So, you should be
upgrading your nodes.

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:30.710
But yeah, the blog post covers
the several different ways

00:33:30.710 --> 00:33:33.950
you can do it and how you
can make your upgrades happen

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:35.467
in a timely fashion.

00:33:35.467 --> 00:33:38.050
It also has some discussion of
things like node pools and what

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:42.380
not, what to do when you
have volumes mounted,

00:33:42.380 --> 00:33:45.160
and how to manage that, and
some other minor points as well.

00:33:45.160 --> 00:33:46.940
It's pretty comprehensive.

00:33:46.940 --> 00:33:48.815
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
I really like the fact

00:33:48.815 --> 00:33:51.360
that the easiest way
to upgrade a node pool

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:56.840
or even a master is to just go
to the information on Google

00:33:56.840 --> 00:34:00.530
on the console, and you
click on upgrade available

00:34:00.530 --> 00:34:01.270
and that's it.

00:34:01.270 --> 00:34:01.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:34:01.630 --> 00:34:03.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is incredibly simple.

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:06.890
But, of course, if you want
to do it through gcloud

00:34:06.890 --> 00:34:08.880
so you can write your
own script or whatever,

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:10.800
that is totally doable
and very simple.

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:13.591
And we also explain how
to do it on the blog post.

00:34:13.591 --> 00:34:14.590
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:34:14.590 --> 00:34:18.050
So, before we run away, I know
that you're not necessarily

00:34:18.050 --> 00:34:20.620
traveling anywhere but you
have a new video series.

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:22.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yes, that is true.

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:25.730
Yeah, there is-- I'm
sure that some of you

